# the x86-64 selection table: rules, constants and the classes that hold values
#
# every instruction is a row of masc's catalog and every class a class of its
# register file. integers, conditions and pointers live in the 64-bit general
# registers and each operation names the view of the width it runs at, eax or
# al or rax of one location, so a value read at several widths is never
# copied between views. a value narrower than 64 bits leaves the bits above
# it undefined, but a condition, which every rule keeps 0 or 1 in the whole
# register. a byte or word operation whose result needs no extension runs at
# 32 bits, whose write ends the whole value, and every narrower write is the
# read and write of a tie, so no result is ever partly written.
#
# x86's two operand forms read and write their first operand, which a rule
# states as a tie of the result to the operand it replaces, and an
# instruction that merges into its destination, as cvtsi2sd, follows a whole
# write of it. the flags are no class: a compare and the branch, set or move
# that reads them are one expansion, and masc's rows state what each reads
# and writes of rflags. division, the high half of a product, a variable shift
# and the string moves read and write fixed registers, which the expansion
# moves its operands into and its results out of.
#
# a cost is two for each instruction an expansion emits, with a multiply
# counting three and a divide twelve. it is less by one for each instruction
# the pattern covers, and by one when the rule takes a constant as an
# immediate or folds an address into a memory operand.
#
# a float constant is loaded from read-only data, which the pool legalisation
# makes before selection, and a constant an expansion needs, as the bounds of a
# saturating conversion, is built in a general register and moved across.

